@page "/projects/portfolio"

<ProjectPage>

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        The site you're reading. It's written in <strong>C#</strong>, Microsoft's programming
        language, and most of it runs inside your browser rather than on a server. A small
        piece runs in <strong>Microsoft's cloud</strong> to receive messages from the contact
        form.
    </p>

    <p>
        Both live at the same web address. That keeps the setup simple and identical
        everywhere it runs: on my computer, in test copies of the site, and live. The two
        halves share a single description of the messages they exchange, so if one side
        changes in a way the other doesn't expect, the mistake is caught before the site can
        be updated, rather than breaking the live site.
    </p>

    <h2>Running for free</h2>

    <p>
        Everything runs on free plans, and I chose them for one shared property:
        <strong>if they're overused, they stop rather than send a bill.</strong> Go past the
        site's monthly traffic allowance and it pauses; the service that keeps the site's
        activity records, its logs, has a daily limit, after which it simply stops
        collecting.
    </p>

    <p>
        That decided how the contact form works. Sending each message as an email seemed
        obvious until I checked the costs: Microsoft's email service charges for every
        message, and SendGrid, a popular alternative, ended its free plan in 2025. A public
        form that sends a paid email every time it's used invites someone to run up the bill.
        So messages are saved in those activity records instead, where misuse can at worst
        stop the service for a while, never cost money.
    </p>

    <h2>Built to withstand misuse</h2>

    <p>
        Anything that runs in your browser can be read and changed by whoever is using it,
        and anyone can send messages straight to the form's server without ever visiting the
        site. So the server treats every message as possibly hostile:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            The same rules check a message in your browser, for quick feedback, and again on
            the server, which <strong>has the final say</strong>, because the browser's check
            can be skipped.
        </li>
        <li>
            Messages have a size limit, checked twice: once against the size the sender says
            the message is, and again while it's actually being read, because the sender
            could be lying.
        </li>
        <li>
            Fields that should be a single line, like your name and email address, can't
            contain line breaks. That blocks a well-known trick for turning forms that send
            email into spam machines, even though this form doesn't send email today.
        </li>
        <li>
            A hidden field that only automated spam bots fill in catches the simplest ones.
            They're told their message was sent, because telling a bot it was caught only
            helps it adapt.
        </li>
        <li>
            The site tells your browser to block any code that doesn't come from the site
            itself, such as code an attacker manages to sneak in. It also switches on other
            standard browser protections.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Built so it can be tested</h2>

    <p>
        <strong>The code that decides what happens to a message is kept apart from the code
        that sends and receives it over the internet.</strong> That way the important rules
        can be tested directly and quickly, without having to run the whole site. When the two
        are mixed, the rules become hard to test, and the tests end up checking very little.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's built</h2>

    <p>
        The code must meet Microsoft's recommended quality rules with not a single warning, or
        the site won't be put together at all. That way the project's standards are enforced
        automatically rather than remembered. Every change has to pass the tests and a
        formatting check before it's accepted, and my own changes get a preview copy of the
        site to look at first. Once accepted, a change is tested, built and published
        automatically.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
